  "knowledgePattern": "Separate the two common-tangent configurations",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Draw radii to the contact points",
      "body": "<p>Read the centres and radii, then use the right angles between each radius and its tangent to find a useful right triangle.</p>",
      "recap": "A radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent; centre distance and radii distinguish circle configurations.",
      "pitfall": "Check that the proposed type of common tangent can exist before calculating its length.",
      "pause": "Find the centre distance and draw the possible positions of the centres relative to the tangent.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l03",
          "reminder": "A radius to a point of tangency is perpendicular to the tangent; centre distance and radii distinguish circle configurations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the perpendicular offsets",
      "body": "<p>When the centres are on the same side of the tangent, compare their distances from it; when they are on opposite sides, combine those distances.</p>",
      "recap": "Pythagoras relates the along-line and perpendicular components to the length between their endpoints.",
      "pitfall": "Different tangent lines can give the same length, and only distinct lengths are requested.",
      "pause": "Calculate the squared lengths for the geometric cases and combine distinct values.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-right-triangle-ratios",
          "reminder": "Pythagoras relates the along-line and perpendicular components to the length between their endpoints."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The centres are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, their distance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mn>12</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>, and the radii are 3 and 4. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, both types of common tangent exist.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> For an external common tangent, both centres lie on the same side of the line. Their perpendicular offsets differ by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so a right triangle gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>15</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>224</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> For an internal common tangent, the centres lie on opposite sides, so the perpendicular offsets add to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>. This gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>15</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>176</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>4.</strong> The two reflected tangents of each type repeat the same length. The distinct lengths therefore contribute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>224</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>176</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow></math>, answer E. The question asks for squared lengths, so there is no need to evaluate their square roots.</p>"
}
